 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476849 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476849 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476849 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476849 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476849 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476849 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186476693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476849 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d512cc49-4e5a-531f-9fa7-3ee72ee1e793" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476867 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476867 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476867 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476867 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476867 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186476626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476867 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476867 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476867 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186476532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476867 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f8ade0a2-d2b7-5d69-add8-6955d332e25b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476876 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476876 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143864" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476876 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476876 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given in the table below. Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}+XY].$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.35$ | $0.2$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476876 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0bf6261a-5734-5922-92db-897115d70061" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476876 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with supports ${S}_{X}$ and ${S}_{Y},$ respectively, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability mass function with support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}.$ If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)={x}^{3}+xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}+XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =({0}^{3}+0\\cdot 1)\\cdot f(0,1)+({0}^{3}+0\\cdot 2)\\cdot f(0,2)+({1}^{3}+1\\cdot 1)\\cdot f(1,1)+({1}^{3}+1\\cdot 2)\\cdot f(1,2) \\\\  & =0\\cdot f(0,1)+0\\cdot f(0,2)+2\\cdot f(1,1)+3\\cdot f(1,2) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.15+0\\cdot 0.3+2\\cdot 0.35+3\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0+0+0.7+0.6 \\\\  & =1.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476882 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476882 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143873" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476882 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476882 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[XY],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476882 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2641e663-5fde-5be8-b7d8-7f4783c01d2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476882 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot (x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{y{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{y}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{6}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476893 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476893 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143881" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476893 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476893 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}{Y}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y+1,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476893 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4b048d9e-f8ea-5592-bd84-57a472dcb0fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476893 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}{Y}^{2}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\cdot (y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({y}^{3}+{y}^{2}){\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({y}^{3}+{y}^{2}){[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{8}{3}({y}^{3}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476897 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476897 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143839" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476897 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476897 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given in the table below. Find $\\text{E}[Y{X}^{2}].$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476897 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "52477810-1cd6-5cba-bcf2-24a5bd23146d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476897 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with supports ${S}_{X}$ and ${S}_{Y},$ respectively, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability mass function with support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}.$ If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=y{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {1}^{2})\\cdot f(1,2)+(4\\cdot {1}^{2})\\cdot f(1,4)+(2\\cdot {2}^{2})\\cdot f(2,2)+(4\\cdot {2}^{2})\\cdot f(2,4) \\\\  & =2\\cdot f(1,2)+4\\cdot f(1,4)+8\\cdot f(2,2)+16\\cdot f(2,4) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.2+4\\cdot 0+8\\cdot 0.7+16\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0.4+0+5.6+1.6 \\\\  & =7.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476907 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476907 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476907 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476907 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given in the table below. Find $\\text{E}[2XY].$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.4$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476907 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a972e15-7a8a-51e9-bb50-f1773b051459" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476907 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with supports ${S}_{X}$ and ${S}_{Y},$ respectively, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability mass function with support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}.$ If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=2xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 1\\cdot 2)\\cdot f(1,2)+(2\\cdot 1\\cdot 4)\\cdot f(1,4)\\,+(2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2)\\cdot f(2,2)+(2\\cdot 2\\cdot 4)\\cdot f(2,4) \\\\  & =4\\cdot f(1,2)+8\\cdot f(1,4)+8\\cdot f(2,2)+16\\cdot f(2,4) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.1+8\\cdot 0.25+8\\cdot 0.25+16\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =0.4+2+2+6.4 \\\\  & =10.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476926 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476926 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143857" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476926 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476926 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given in the table below. Find $\\text{E}[(Y-X)^{2}].$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.55$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476926 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "960f4c07-bbdc-52c9-b625-5804d6b3ddf5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476926 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with supports ${S}_{X}$ and ${S}_{Y},$ respectively, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability mass function with support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}.$ If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=(y-x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[(Y-X)^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1-2)^{2}\\cdot f(2,1)+{(2-2)}^{2}\\cdot f(2,2)+(1-4)^{2}\\cdot f(4,1)+(2-4)^{2}\\cdot f(4,2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot f(2,1)+0\\cdot f(2,2)+9\\cdot f(4,1)+4\\cdot f(4,2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.1+0\\cdot 0.55+9\\cdot 0.15+4\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.1+0+1.35+0.8 \\\\  & =2.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476927 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476927 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476927 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476927 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[X{Y}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}8xy,\\, & 0\\le x,\\,0\\le y,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476927 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9a9fb34d-c302-5432-923d-53ca78af43c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476927 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x,\\,0\\le y,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/df/dfa15482cfa0e8fdc9e98b05a71d2b64a89b3886da44f6966fee9f1b5e096eef.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X{Y}^{2}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}x{y}^{2}\\cdot 8xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}{(1-{x}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{7}{x}^{7}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =2[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{7}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{105}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476945 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476945 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476945 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476945 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given in the table below. Find $\\text{E}[(X+Y)^{2}].$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476945 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b01bd86d-94ac-51f4-ac36-7b94992125f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476945 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with supports ${S}_{X}$ and ${S}_{Y},$ respectively, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability mass function with support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}.$ If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[(X+Y)^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =(0+1)^{2}\\cdot f(0,1)+(0+2)^{2}\\cdot f(0,2)+(1+1)^{2}\\cdot f(1,1)+(1+2)^{2}\\cdot f(1,2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot f(0,1)+4\\cdot f(0,2)+4\\cdot f(1,1)+9\\cdot f(1,3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.2+4\\cdot 0.1+4\\cdot 0.2+9\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.2+0.4+0.8+4.5 \\\\  & =5.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476949 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476949 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476949 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476949 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[Y\\sqrt{X}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2{y}^{3}},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476949 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b80e305c-0467-5dea-919a-dc17751b8238" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476949 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=y\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,1\\le y\\le 2\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\sqrt{X}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}y\\sqrt{x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4[-\\frac{1}{y}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476960 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476960 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143875" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476960 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476960 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[Y\\operatorname{cos}⁡X],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476960 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb839c9a-7589-5685-a16a-88269e423380" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476960 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\operatorname{cos}⁡X] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}{[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476967 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476967 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143905" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476967 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476967 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{{e}^{X-Y}}{1-X}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le 1,, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476967 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df4b9bd0-fd66-55ed-92b5-c754afe8ffc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476967 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{x-y}}{1-x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le 1\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/bc/bc44d4c99cf60148685a99b5c3b5ea2e732ea4b9939aefe5ba465d46e92c5ea8.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{{e}^{X-Y}}{1-X}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}\\frac{{e}^{x-y}}{1-x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{e}^{x-y}}{1-x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x-y}}{1-x}\\cdot {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-x}{\\int }_{x}^{1}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-x}\\cdot [y]_{y=x}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-x}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{e}^{x}]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476969 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476969 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476969 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476969 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(X-Y)],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}},\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le y\\le x; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476969 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e42bb61d-9253-506b-85c5-8f5b23f330ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476969 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y).\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le y\\le x\\},$ where $f(x)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/ff/ff35a5c562607b4ac314d3ae755156d75baf5d8b01b813f6b59d67dac540e154.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(X-Y)] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)]_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)]}_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})] \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{\\pi }^{2}}(1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{{\\pi }^{2}}(2-\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476973 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476973 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476973 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{Y}{7X}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{21}{4}{x}^{2}y,\\, & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476973 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1049e31-fa99-5f07-ad8f-6d47af518d90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476973 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\frac{y}{7x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/54/54777bc840acd1d1c24da08ccb99025b039af9c47421602a3d9dd73df2a91779.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{Y}{7X}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}\\frac{y}{7x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{7x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\frac{y}{7x}\\cdot \\frac{21}{4}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{3}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x-{x}^{7})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}]}_{x=-1}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8})-(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
